<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anouvong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1767–1829</span> · Also known as Chao Anouvong, Chao Anou, Xaiya Setthathirath V</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anouvong was the last king of Vientiane, ruling from 1805 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Siam. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-si-saket">Wat Si Saket</a> and other monuments and sought to restore Lao independence. In 1826 he rebelled and marched towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, but his armies were defeated, Vientiane was sacked in 1827 and its people deported. Captured in 1828, he died a prisoner in Bangkok, and he is honoured as a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anouvong.jpg" alt="Anouvong" width="229" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User: Chaoborus · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StadAnouvong-entrance-2011_(version_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
